  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "atomId": "atom-1",
      "spans": [
        {
          "id": "doc-cd2ffd52140b::sentence-6",
          "documentId": "doc-cd2ffd52140b",
          "text": "Non-traded\tgoods\tare\ttypically\tcheaper\tin\tpoorer\teconomies,\tso\tthat\tPPPs\tare\ttypically\tlower\tthan\texchange\trates\tfor\tpoor\tcountries,\tand\tare\tmore\tso\tthe\tpoorer\tthe\tcountry\n\n",
          "start": 592,
          "end": 765,
          "context": "Purchasing\tpower\tparity\texchange\trates,\tPPPs,\tare\tinternational\tmultilateral\tprice\tindexes\tthat\tmeasure,\tfor\tthe\tvarious\tcomponents\tof\tGDP,\tthe\tamount\tof\tlocal\tcurrency\trequired\tto\tpurchase\tthe\tsame\treal\tamount\tin\tthat\tcountry\trelative\tto\ta\tnumeraire,\twhich\tis\ttypically\tthe\tUnited\tStates.\t Non-traded\tgoods\tare\ttypically\tcheaper\tin\tpoorer\teconomies,\tso\tthat\tPPPs\tare\ttypically\tlower\tthan\texchange\trates\tfor\tpoor\tcountries,\tand\tare\tmore\tso\tthe\tpoorer\tthe\tcountry\n\n RECOVERED SOURCE TEXT\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-07b5051e0f4b::sentence-156",
          "documentId": "doc-07b5051e0f4b",
          "text": "This approach does not need to directly measure pass-through for exports and imports since it makes use of average prices received for domestic production and domestic inputs used in the production process.\n",
          "start": 22294,
          "end": 22501,
          "context": "The first approach to calculating producing power parities uses commodity output and input prices for individual industries to create deflators for industry output (value added).  This approach does not need to directly measure pass-through for exports and imports since it makes use of average prices received for domestic production and domestic inputs used in the production process.\n One set of these studies estimates producing power parities using commodity data coming directly from firm-based surveys. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-4480543c9460::sentence-12",
          "documentId": "doc-4480543c9460",
          "text": "The average value for Nigeria during that period was 4098.47 U.S. dollars with a minimum of 2901.77 U.S. dollars in 1995 and a maximum of 5516.39 U.S. dollars in 2014. ",
          "start": 498,
          "end": 666,
          "context": "For that indicator, we provide data for Nigeria from 1990 to 2021.  The average value for Nigeria during that period was 4098.47 U.S. dollars with a minimum of 2901.77 U.S. dollars in 1995 and a maximum of 5516.39 U.S. dollars in 2014.  The latest value from 2021 is 4969.22 U.S. dollars. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-4480543c9460::sentence-26",
          "documentId": "doc-4480543c9460",
          "text": "The average value for Nigeria during that period was 4056.42 U.S. dollars with a minimum of 2895.35 U.S. dollars in 1995 and a maximum of 5429.1 U.S. dollars in 2015.\n",
          "start": 1129,
          "end": 1296,
          "context": "For that indicator, we provide data for Nigeria from 1990 to 2021.  The average value for Nigeria during that period was 4056.42 U.S. dollars with a minimum of 2895.35 U.S. dollars in 1995 and a maximum of 5429.1 U.S. dollars in 2015.\n The latest value from 2021 is 4922.63 U.S. dollars. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-07b5051e0f4b::sentence-308",
          "documentId": "doc-07b5051e0f4b",
          "text": "Measuring a producing power parity is more difficult than measuring a purchasing power parity because statistical systems are not pre-disposed to gathering detailed, internationally comparable, estimates of import and export commodities in a manner analogous to the data collected for purchasing power estimates that need only make use of readily available expenditure-based prices.\n",
          "start": 47790,
          "end": 48173,
          "context": "7.2 Level of producing power parities from projectors\n Measuring a producing power parity is more difficult than measuring a purchasing power parity because statistical systems are not pre-disposed to gathering detailed, internationally comparable, estimates of import and export commodities in a manner analogous to the data collected for purchasing power estimates that need only make use of readily available expenditure-based prices.\n Nevertheless, using the gross output and intermediate inputs for calculating a producing power parity, Baldwin, Gu and Yan (2008) generate a U.S.-Canada producing power estimate of 0.83 for the business sector and 0.85 for total GDP in 1999. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-07b5051e0f4b::sentence-33",
          "documentId": "doc-07b5051e0f4b",
          "text": "Purchasing power parity comparisons deflate the relative levels of nominal income (often referred to as gross domestic product [GDP]) using price indexes derived from prices for final domestic expenditures (primarily consumption, investment and government expenditures). ",
          "start": 2278,
          "end": 2549,
          "context": "Indices of purchasing power parity are used to compare real income levels across countries based on a nation's ability to buy goods and services.  Purchasing power parity comparisons deflate the relative levels of nominal income (often referred to as gross domestic product [GDP]) using price indexes derived from prices for final domestic expenditures (primarily consumption, investment and government expenditures).  When domestic prices, rather than domestic and traded prices, are used to calculate real income, the resulting measure is referred to as real gross domestic income (GDI).\n"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
